deliberation_thread.body:
@Argh4k Thanks for sharing your findings. It appears to be a bug where we might not consider the new NNN in updatePod. When the first API call is synchronous and there are no network delays, this line should retrieve the pod with NNN set and correctly evaluate `if nnnNeedsUpdate`:

https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/b08fa0cdf18610be74d9741cf361446a5f42ff76/pkg/scheduler/schedule_one.go#L1229

However, this may not work for async API calls or in real clusters. It looks like we need to add a line that sets NNN on a pod or checks `if nnnNeedsUpdate` in a different way.

@macsko I think there is one more bug in the logic that you mentioned. The new pod info based on cachedPod is assigned to podInfo.PodInfo here: https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/b08fa0cdf18610be74d9741cf361446a5f42ff76/pkg/scheduler/schedule_one.go#L1243

But actually the pod that we are sending to the updatePod is a pod extracted earlier: https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/b08fa0cdf18610be74d9741cf361446a5f42ff76/pkg/scheduler/schedule_one.go#L1205

So the "pod" is pointing to old pod data.

> I think I have a fix in [#137462](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/137462).

Fix is merged and CI indicates this fixes all of the affected jobs. I think the next scheduled periodic runs of those jobs are coming up.

@BenTheElder Are we good to close this issue or should we keep it open in case the fix needs to be backported?

> filed follow-up [#137503](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/137503)

@BenTheElder  thanks for opening the follow-up; 

Is it confirmed that the "stopped accepting POST" is exclusive to the [query](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/137462/files#diff-9b2eaad633b1ac2ad543e05ddeb831f207bfb4d034456070bc7d32ea45b7ed05R624) feature, or is this a behavior inherited from the [logs](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/137462/files#diff-9b2eaad633b1ac2ad543e05ddeb831f207bfb4d034456070bc7d32ea45b7ed05L623) endpoint

CVE-2026-25679 in kubectl

### What happened?

Trivy is reporting that the latest kubectl stable version is vulnerable to CVE-2026-25679

### What did you expect to happen?

Not being vulnerable

### How can we reproduce it (as minimally and precisely as possible)?

Scan the kubectl binary using Trivy

Here is a copy of the report for the latest stable kubectl version
